<section id="saltwater-demo" class="saltwater-lab" aria-labelledby="tank-title">
  <div class="tank-heading"><div><p class="folio-kicker">BUOYANCY & CIRCULATION</p><h2 id="tank-title">A drop of saltwater</h2><p>Denser water sinks and pulls its surroundings into motion. A fresher drop rises. The curled edges belong to the water itself.</p></div><button id="tank-present" type="button">Present the tank ↗</button></div>
  <div class="tank-layout">
    <div class="tank-stage">
      <canvas id="dye-tank-canvas" width="1024" height="768" tabindex="0" aria-label="Interactive water tank. Click or drag to release dye, or press Enter to release a drop." aria-describedby="tank-instruction"></canvas>
      <canvas id="tank-flow-canvas" width="1024" height="768" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <div class="tank-corner"><span id="tank-view-label">Dye</span><output id="tank-clock">0.0 s</output></div>
      <div class="tank-ruler"><span>0</span><span>12</span><span>24 cm</span></div>
      <div class="tank-start" id="tank-start" hidden><button id="tank-start-button" type="button">Run the tank ▷</button></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tank-controls">
      <div class="tank-presets" role="group" aria-label="Tank experiments"><button type="button" data-tank-preset="salt" aria-pressed="true">Salty drop</button><button type="button" data-tank-preset="fresh" aria-pressed="false">Fresh drop</button><button type="button" data-tank-preset="layers" aria-pressed="false">Layered water</button></div>
      <label>Water salinity <output id="tank-water-value">30 g/kg</output><input id="tank-water" type="range" min="0" max="40" step="1" value="30"/></label>
      <label>Drop salinity <output id="tank-drop-value">36 g/kg</output><input id="tank-drop" type="range" min="0" max="40" step="1" value="36"/></label>
      <p id="tank-density-meaning" class="tank-meaning">The drop is denser than the water.</p>
      <label>Drop radius <output id="tank-radius-value">7 mm</output><input id="tank-radius" type="range" min="3" max="12" step="1" value="7"/></label>
      <div class="tank-selects"><label>Show<select id="tank-view"><option value="dye">Dye</option><option value="salinity">Salinity</option><option value="vorticity">Rotation</option></select></label><label>Playback<select id="tank-speed"><option value="0.5">½×</option><option value="1" selected>1×</option><option value="2">2×</option></select></label></div>
      <label class="tank-check"><input id="tank-flow" type="checkbox"/> Water motion</label>
      <div class="tank-buttons"><button id="tank-drop-button" type="button">Release dye ↓</button><button id="tank-pause" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Pause</button><button id="tank-reset" type="button">Reset</button></div>
      <p id="tank-instruction" class="tank-caption">Click to release a drop, or drag to pour a ribbon of dye. Changing the water salinity starts a new tank.</p>
      <p id="tank-status" class="tank-caption" role="status">Gold marks a saltier or matching addition; coral marks a fresher one.</p>
      <div id="tank-legend" class="tank-legend" hidden><span id="tank-legend-low"></span><canvas id="tank-legend-ramp" width="256" height="8" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span id="tank-legend-high"></span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="tank-explanation">The drop begins underwater without a push. As it moves, it draws surrounding water inward and rolls the edges of its plume. In the ocean, dense water can descend along the seabed and entrain lighter water on the way.</p>
  <details class="tank-model"><summary>What the physics includes</summary><p>A two-dimensional incompressible fluid calculation runs entirely in your browser. Salt changes buoyancy; dye follows the calculated velocity. The tank is 24 × 18 cm, with glass sides and a bottom that resist sliding, and a flat surface with zero shear. TEOS-10 supplies seawater density at 10 °C and atmospheric pressure. The controls use salinity in g/kg; the separate TEOS-10 calculator below uses Practical Salinity.</p><p>The 256 × 128 grid resolves 0.94 × 1.41 mm cells. Effective diffusion and numerical smoothing are larger than molecular salt diffusion. This illustrates buoyancy and plume circulation; it does not reproduce three-dimensional turbulence, a free-surface drop impact, or any particular ocean current. Error-corrected transport preserves the calculated curls; conservative higher-order transport limits how much dye is blurred. The screen blends calculated frames without adding physical detail. Gold/coral intensity is a nonlinear display of passive pigment. Salinity uses a fixed 0–40 g/kg scale; rotation uses −5 to +5 s⁻¹ in the screen's downward-y coordinates.</p><p>Salt and dye transfers are conserved at cell faces. The calculation is also checked against an exact decaying vortex and a translated dye patch. Projection keeps the calculated flow nearly divergence-free. Matching-salinity and stable-layer tests remain at rest, and fixed inputs replay identically. <a href="/hidden-rivers/dye-tank/model.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Equations, boundaries and verification ↗</a> · <a href="/hidden-rivers/dye-tank/verification.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numerical results ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://www.teos-10.org/pubs/gsw/html/gsw_rho_t_exact.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TEOS-10 seawater density ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~rbridson/fluidsimulation/fluids_notes.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bridson's fluid simulation notes ↗</a></p></details>
</section>
